Notebook[{
Cell["PadicDigits", "ObjectName",ExpressionUUID->"f34eb8d7-6487-4022-87a0-0f5f2cf02e3c"],

Cell["\<\
PadicDigits[s] gives the stored integer digits of an exact or \
finite-precision object from highest to lowest place, before display-symbol \
conversion. Positive valuations append zero digits; negative valuations do \
not insert a radix point or padding. Zero gives {0}. PadicDigits[r,p] first \
constructs the exact expansion of rational r. For a finite approximation, an \
explicit p must match its stored base and preserves its known digits. Lazy \
objects return preview digits; use PadicN[s,p,n] to request a specific \
precision.\
\>", "Usage",ExpressionUUID->"f0bb1573-d3cd-492a-9735-9824f9d532fc"],

Cell["\<\
Digit data remains integer-valued even when the formatted expansion uses \
letters or separated decimal digits. The list runs from highest to lowest \
place.\
\>", "Notes",ExpressionUUID->"da50d898-49b5-42b5-bb0d-2c46a18be0e1"],

Cell["\<\
A positive valuation appends zeros. A negative valuation does not insert a \
point or fractional padding. Read the object's valuation separately to locate \
its powers.\
\>", "Notes",ExpressionUUID->"7cd7fd2d-2e67-471d-961b-e7e90e7aa06f"],

Cell["\<\
An explicit base preserves an approximation's known digits when the base \
matches, and rejects a different base. Zero always gives {0}, so this list \
does not convey a zero's precision.\
\>", "Notes",ExpressionUUID->"92378a33-8a9d-4164-ac70-06cc382bd526"],

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{"Needs", "[", "\"\<PadicNumbers`\>\"", 
  "]"}]], "ExampleInitialization",ExpressionUUID->"dc2ef8fb-5d9c-48fa-9329-\
990da3047e23"],

